# Local-only path to a service account key, never the key itself --
# still not something to commit (points at where a live GCP credential
# lives on this machine).
.claude/skills/*/keypath.local.txt
*.local.txt
*.local.json

build/
dist/
node_modules/
.vscode/
.firebase/

# Sandbox-only persona credentials — generated at activation, never committed.
*credentials.local.json

# EOS network telemetry (Phase 4) — raw machine-local logs must never be committed (see docs/orchestration/network-telemetry.md §13)
netwatch-standalone.csv
netwatch-full.csv
netwatch.pid
netwatch.log
**/netwatch-*.csv
.mcp.json

# Operator access-intent / result records written by
# functions/scripts/operatorAccessCommand.js. These are deliberately LOCAL
# operator-side evidence (mode 0600), never governed objects, and they carry
# principal UIDs -- they must never be committed.
functions/.operator-access-intents/
.operator-access-intents/

# Emulator debug output — generated at runtime, never a repo artifact.
firestore-debug.log
firebase-debug.log
.impeccable/

# Emulator E2E harness — generated port-overridden firebase.json (functions/scripts/runE2eEmulatorSuite.mjs).
.e2e-emulator-firebase.json

# Per-session cache written by the impeccable design hook. It records absolute
# local paths and session UUIDs, so it is machine-specific by construction and
# has no meaning in another checkout. It was tracked by accident and has been
# committed several times as incidental noise; worse, it shows as a dirty file
# constantly, which is exactly the kind of always-there diff people learn to
# ignore -- and the production release runbook refuses a dirty tree.
**/.impeccable/hook.cache.json

# CERTIFICATION SCRATCH AND EVIDENCE, generated by the UX/reachability certification tooling
# (orphan reports, reachability JSON, CSS restore experiments). Machine- and run-specific: it says
# what one pass over one checkout found, and it is regenerated rather than reviewed.
#
# It was never tracked, and being untracked was the problem: `git add -A` in a product PR swept in
# nineteen of these files -- roughly 15,000 lines of scratch -- alongside a seven-file fix. A
# reviewer reading that diff cannot tell the change from the noise, and the noise is where a real
# edit goes unnoticed.
#
# Deliberately narrow: this one directory, not a wildcard over artefacts in general. Existing files
# are left on disk untouched; ignoring them is about what future commits can pick up, not about
# deleting anyone's working output.
field-ops-app-vite/.certification/

# Release logs are transient operator output, not repository artifacts.
.release.log
.m.log
.emu.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local operator payloads (root-anchored)
#
# These sit untracked at the repository root on operator machines and are NOT repository content.
# None of them was ignored before, so `git add -A` would have committed them -- which is how a
# production `parts` read-back ended up under docs/audits/ in the first place.
#
# Every pattern below is anchored with a leading slash. `conflicts.csv` and `invalid-rows.csv` are
# legitimately committed under docs/audits/, and an unanchored rule would silently shadow them.
#
# Ignoring is not deleting. Migration evidence, audit decks and role matrices may carry business or
# legal value, and removing them is an owner decision.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Generated: emulator and run logs, and the bundles produced beside them.
/firestore-debug.log
/firestore-debug.*.log
/*-terminal.log
/inventory-effects-production-detection-*
/*.tar.gz
/*.tar.gz.sha256
/*.tar.gz(*).sha256

# Operator evidence and working documents. Untracked deliberately; owner-owned.
/Taylor-Migration-Evidence/
/Sandbox_CRM_and_Role_Audit_*/
/Sandbox_CRM_and_Role_Audit_*.ndjson
/Users/
/Hierarchy.xlsx
/Do not ask me to inspect Firestore.docx
